{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 11, "model_name": "mistralai/mistral-small-4-119b-2603", "content": "**Summary of the Wikipedia page \"Raymond III Count of Tripoli\"**\n\nRaymond III was Count of Tripoli from 1152 to 1187. He was the son of Raymond II of Tripoli and Hodierna of Jerusalem. Raymond III became count at a young age after his father was assassinated in 1152. His mother Hodierna ruled as regent until he came of age.\n\n*highlighted section part 1*\n\nRaymond III played a significant role in the politics of the Crusader states. He was a key figure in the conflict between King Amalric I of Jerusalem and Prince Bohemond III of Antioch. Raymond supported Amalric and helped him secure the throne. He also mediated disputes between the Crusader states and the Byzantine Empire.\n\nIn 1164 Raymond was captured by Nur ad-Din at the Battle of Harim and held captive for over a decade. During his captivity Tripoli was ruled by his cousin Aimery of Lusignan. Raymond was released in 1177 after a ransom was paid. Upon his return he resumed his role as count and continued to be involved in the politics of the region.\n\n*highlighted section part 2*\n\nRaymond III was a patron of the Knights Hospitaller and other military orders. He donated lands and resources to support their activities. He also played a role in the foundation of the Order of the Holy Sepulchre in 1172. Raymond was known for his piety and support of the Church.\n\nIn 1187 Raymond III played a crucial role in the lead-up to the Battle of Hattin. He advised King Guy of Lusignan against confronting Saladin directly. However his advice was ignored and the Crusader army was destroyed. Raymond barely escaped the battle and died shortly afterward on 10 December 1187.\n\n*highlighted section part 3*\n\nRaymond III was married twice first to Eschiva of Bures with whom he had no children and second to Teodora Komnene a Byzantine princess. He had no legitimate heirs and was succeeded by his 